// Moderation (localized blocks)
"moderation_intro_text" = "According to the 2020-2025 Dietary Guidelines for Americans, certain individuals should not consume alcohol. It’s safest to avoid alcohol altogether if you are:\n\n";
"moderation_primary_reason_1" = "Taking medications that interact with alcohol";
"moderation_primary_reason_2" = "Managing a medical condition that can be made worse by drinking";
"moderation_primary_reason_3" = "Under the age of 21, the minimum legal drinking age in the United States";
"moderation_primary_reason_4" = "Recovering from alcohol use disorder (AUD) or unable to control the amount you drink";
"moderation_primary_reason_5" = "Pregnant or might be pregnant";
"moderation_full_text" = "In addition, certain individuals, particularly older adults, who are planning to drive a vehicle or operate machinery—or who are participating in activities that require skill, coordination, and alertness—should avoid alcohol completely.\n\nWhat is alcohol misuse?\n\nNIAAA defines heavy drinking as follows:\n\nFor women:\n4 or more drinks on any day or 8 or more per week.\n\nFor men:\n5 or more drinks on any day or 15 or more per week.";
"moderation_target_women" = "4 or more drinks on any day or 8 or more per week.";
"moderation_target_men" = "5 or more drinks on any day or 15 or more per week.";

// Hold your liquor
"hold_your_liquor_header" = "Why is being able to “hold your liquor” a concern?";
"hold_your_liquor_body_paragraph_1" = "For some people who drink, it takes quite a few drinks to “get a buzz” or feel relaxed, and they may be less likely to show signs of intoxication compared to others. This is sometimes called being able to “hold your liquor” or “drink someone under the table.”";
"hold_your_liquor_body_paragraph_2" = "Often these individuals are unaware that their body’s lower response to the intoxicating effects of alcohol isn’t protection from alcohol problems but instead is a reason for caution.";
"hold_your_liquor_body_paragraph_3" = "These individuals tend to drink more, socialize with people who drink a lot, and develop a tolerance to alcohol (i.e., it takes more and more alcohol to feel or act intoxicated). As a result, they have an increased risk for developing AUD. Someone who misuses alcohol, especially over the long-term, can experience permanent liver, heart, or brain damage. And all people who drink, regardless of the amount, need to be aware that critical decision-making abilities and driving-related skills are already diminished long before a person shows physical signs of intoxication.";

// Tobacco (smoking basic knowledge)
"tobacco_header" = "What is tobacco?";
"tobacco_body_paragraph_1" = "Tobacco comes from the leaves of the Nicotiana plant and is used in things like cigarettes, cigars, and chewing tobacco. The main thing in tobacco is nicotine, a natural substance that can be addictive and affect your brain";
"tobacco_body_paragraph_2" = "When people smoke cigarettes, they inhale over 7,000 chemicals, and nicotine is the one that makes smoking addictive. There are also other chemicals added to make the smoke taste better and for the nicotine to be absorbed more easily.";
"tobacco_body_paragraph_3" = "Each time someone smokes a cigarette, they get about 1-2 milligrams of nicotine, which quickly goes to their brain and makes their heart beat faster, raises their blood pressure, and increases their breathing. It's kind of like a quick energy boost, but it can become a habit.";
"tobacco_body_paragraph_4" = "Cigarettes are designed so that someone who smokes regularly gets around 200 \"hits\" of nicotine to their brain every day. Other tobacco products, like chewing tobacco, also have nicotine. Some people use e-cigarettes to get nicotine without all the extra chemicals in tobacco smoke.";
"tobacco_body_paragraph_5" = "Nicotine works on the brain in a way that makes it feel good and can become a habit because it gives a sense of pleasure. That's why it's important to understand what's in tobacco and the effects it can have on your body and brain.";

// Vaping (smoking basic knowledge)
"vaping_header" = "How is vaping (E-cigarettes) safer than tobacco?";
"vaping_body_paragraph_1" = "E-cigarettes go by many names like \"e-cigs,\" vapes,\" and \"vape pens.\" They can look like regular cigarettes or even things like USB flash drives and pens.";
"vaping_body_paragraph_2" = "These devices run on batteries and let people inhale something called aerosol. Vaping became popular because some thought it might be a less harmful choice compared to regular smoking.";
"vaping_body_paragraph_3" = "Inside that aerosol, there's usually nicotine (though not always), flavors, and some other chemicals. The devices can be rechargeable, disposable, or come with tanks and mods.";
"vaping_body_paragraph_4" = "Is it safer than tobacco?\nE-cigarette aerosol generally contains fewer toxic chemicals than the deadly mix of 7,000 chemicals in smoke from regular cigarettes. However, e-cigarette aerosol is not harmless. It can contain harmful and potentially harmful substances, including nicotine, heavy metals like lead, volatile organic compounds and cancer-causing agents.";
"vaping_reference_url" = "https://www.cdc.gov/tobacco/basic_information/e-cigarettes/pdfs/Electronic-Cigarettes-Infographic-p.pdf";

// Smoking relax (smoking basic knowledge)
"smoking_relax_header" = "Why does smoking seem to relax me?";
"smoking_relax_body_paragraph_1" = "Nicotine does things in your brain that make you feel good and more awake. It releases chemicals like dopamine, norepinephrine, and serotonin, which are responsible for those \"feeling good\" and \"being awake\" vibes. Nicotine also gives you a quick boost of endorphins, which can help you relax and ease any pains.";
"smoking_relax_body_paragraph_2" = "That's why some people, both adults and teens, use smoking to kind of \"reset\" their mood, emotions, and attention. Especially for those who struggle with anxiety or feeling down, smoking might be a way they try to make themselves feel better. But it's important to know that there are healthier ways to deal with these feelings.";

// Smoking and mental health (smoking basic knowledge)
"smoking_mental_health_header" = "How does smoking affect your mental health?";
"smoking_mental_health_body_paragraph_1" = "Many people think smoking relieves stress. However, Nicotine does something interesting in your brain – it releases a chemical called dopamine. Dopamine is like a happiness signal and makes you feel good. Some people with depression have low levels of dopamine, so they might smoke to get a quick boost.";
"smoking_mental_health_body_paragraph_2" = "But here's the problem... once your brain gets used to nicotine boosters, it will cry for more smoking when it is not getting enough nicotine. Thus, your concentration goes down, your heart beats faster, and you get more anxious and stressed.";
"smoking_mental_health_body_paragraph_3" = "As a result, you smoke to reduce your nicotine stress, not your real stress. This is a vicious cycle that affects your anxiety and mood.";
"smoking_mental_health_body_paragraph_4" = "Depression is common because when you smoke a lot, your brain kind of switches off its own way of making dopamine. So, in the long run, your brain has less dopamine that affects your mood.";

// Moderate drinking education screens
"moderate_drinking_body_1" = "You're doing a fantastic job maintaining moderation with your drinking! As you continue this healthy habit, remember to build other wellness practices like regular exercise, a balanced diet, and enough sleep.";
"moderate_drinking_body_2" = "Focus on managing stress without turning to alcohol or quick fixes. Taking care of your body and mind will help you stay on track and reduce the urge to drink as a stress reliever.";
"moderate_drinking_body_3" = "Keep up the great work—you're laying the groundwork for a healthier, happier life!";
"moderate_drinking_body_4_full" = "Feel free to use My progress section to track your drinking habit if you'd like to.";
"moderate_everyday_body_1" = "I'm proud of you setting a limit to alcohol consumption! You're taking a really important first step by recognizing your daily reliance on alcohol, and that's something to be proud of.";
"moderate_everyday_body_2_full" = "We will show you small but significant changes in Make a plan section.";
"moderate_social_binge_body_1" = "I'm proud of you for recognizing the need to control your drinking pattern! Acknowledging your drinking patterns is a crucial first step toward healthier habits.";
"moderate_social_binge_body_2" = "It's important to reflect on your drinking habits, especially during social events, and consider how you can enjoy those moments without going overboard. By taking small steps to reduce your consumption, you'll start to see the benefits for both your health and your relationships.";
"moderate_social_binge_body_3_full" = "In the Make a plan section, we'll guide you through practical strategies to help you make these changes. You've got this!";
"moderate_problematic_body_1" = "I'm proud of you for recognizing the need to address your heavy drinking! Acknowledging that your drinking patterns may be problematic is a crucial first step toward a healthier life.";
"moderate_problematic_body_2" = "It's essential to reflect on how your alcohol consumption impacts your well-being and relationships. By committing to change and taking small steps to reduce your drinking, you can begin to experience the benefits of a more balanced lifestyle.";
"moderate_problematic_body_3_full" = "In the Make a plan section, we'll guide you through practical strategies to help you manage your drinking more effectively. Remember, you're not alone in this journey!";

// Make a plan - ChangeCell / DefineMakeCell
"make_plan_change_full_text" = "Thinking about a change?\n\nWe want you to know that the decision to reduce alcohol/drug consumption is entirely yours!\n\nBut If you find yourself consistently exceeding recommended guidelines for alcohol consumption, it would be wise to consider stopping, cutting down, or reducing your drinking.\nMixed feelings are normal.\n\nHave you thought about the pros and cons of doing so?";
"make_plan_change_highlight_1" = "the decision to reduce alcohol/drug consumption is entirely yours!";
"make_plan_change_highlight_2" = "stopping, cutting down, or reducing your drinking.";
"define_make_full_text" = "First of all, let's define what is the best for you.\n\nIf you're considering changing your drinking, you'll need to decide whether to cut down or quit. It's a good idea to discuss different options with a healthcare professional, a friend, or someone else you trust.\n\nPlease note, when someone who has been drinking heavily for a prolonged period of time suddenly stops drinking, the body can go into a painful or even potentially life-threatening process of withdrawal.\n\nSymptoms can include nausea, rapid heart rate, seizures, or other problems. Seek medical help to plan a safe recovery. Doctors can prescribe medications to address these symptoms and make the process safer and less distressing.";
"define_make_highlight_1" = "a painful or even potentially life-threatening process of withdrawal.";
"define_make_highlight_2" = "nausea, rapid heart rate, seizures, or other problems.";

