// Ready-to-quit symptom modals (Swift UI)
"quit_symptom_irritability_title" = "Irritability and Mood Swings";
"quit_symptom_difficulty_title" = "Difficulty Concentrating";
"quit_symptom_sleep_title" = "Sleep Disturbances";
"quit_symptom_depression_title" = "Depression and Anxiety";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_intro" = "When you're trying to quit smoking, pretty much everyone feels the urge to smoke from time to time. It can be a small feeling or sometimes really strong. But guess what?\n\nDealing with these cravings is a big deal for staying smoke-free.\n\nHere are some ways to handle it:";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_medicines_heading" = "Use quit-smoking medicines";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_patch_combo" = "A nicotine patch plus a lozenge (mints/cough drops) or gum is the most common combination and you can get them at any drug store.";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_timeline_patch" = "Put on a new patch each morning to get a steady level of nicotine that will reduce your cravings and withdrawal symptoms throughout the day.";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_timeline_fast_acting" = "Use a fast-acting nicotine medicine like lozenges or gum to quickly combat cravings";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_timeline_control" = "You can control how often you use the fast-acting medicine, so you won't get more nicotine than you want.";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_timeline_early_start" = "It's OK to start nicotine patches, gum, or lozenges a week or two before you quit smoking. This can help you get ready and may cut down on your smoking. Starting medicine, especially the nicotine patch, before you quit may improve your chances of quitting successfully.";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_also_try_full" = "You can also try…\n\nOne non-nicotine medicine (prescription from your doctor is required). Varenicline is a pill that works differently from other medicines. It does not contain nicotine. It works by attaching to the same parts of your brain that are stimulated by nicotine. This means that nicotine from a cigarette has fewer places to attach because the varenicline is already there. This makes it harder to get a nicotine \"buzz\".";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_timeline_reduces_urge" = "Reduces your urge to smoke and enjoyment of cigarettes.";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_timeline_strongest_chance" = "Gives you the strongest chance for success of any single medicine.";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_timeline_start_early" = "Can start a week or more before you quit smoking.";
"quit_symptom_nicotine_triggers_full" = "Know your triggers:\n\nCertain things can make you want to smoke, like being around friends you used to smoke with or being in a place where you often smoked. Even memories or feelings can bring it on.\n\nPositive thoughts:\n\nRemember why you decided to quit. You're in control, and cravings don't last forever. They'll pass.";
"quit_symptom_irritability_intro" = "When you're trying to quit smoking, it's totally normal to feel a bit irritated or grouchy. Even folks who never smoked get it - it's just part of the quitting process. So, don't worry, you're not alone in feeling this way.\n\nHere's how to deal with it:";
"quit_symptom_irritability_remind_heading" = "Remind yourself it's normal";
"quit_symptom_irritability_remind_body" = "Feeling grumpy is just your body adjusting to not having nicotine.";
"quit_symptom_irritability_breaths_heading" = "Take deep breaths";
"quit_symptom_irritability_breaths_body" = "When irritation hits, take a few deep breaths to calm yourself. You can find them in \"exercise\".";
"quit_symptom_irritability_why_heading" = "Remember why you quit";
"quit_symptom_irritability_why_body" = "Think about why you decided to quit. It's a great motivator to push through the grumpy moments.";
"quit_symptom_difficulty_intro" = "Right after you quit smoking, you might find it a bit tough to concentrate. Don't worry, it happens to a lot of people.\n\nHere's what you can do:";
"quit_symptom_difficulty_kind_heading" = "Be kind to yourself";
"quit_symptom_difficulty_kind_body" = "Give yourself a break, especially in the first few days after quitting.";
"quit_symptom_difficulty_easy_heading" = "Take it easy";
"quit_symptom_difficulty_easy_body" = "If you can, try not to do things that need a lot of focus right away.";
"quit_symptom_appetite_intro" = "When you quit smoking, it's totally normal for your appetite to go up a bit. Your body might not burn calories as fast, and you might find yourself eating more, maybe because quitting can be a bit stressful, or you just want something to do with your hands and mouth. Plus, food might taste even better since your sense of smell and taste aren't dulled by smoke\n\nHere's what you can do to manage it:";
"quit_symptom_appetite_snacking_heading" = "Smart snacking";
"quit_symptom_appetite_snacking_body" = "Choose healthy snacks like celery, carrots, or sugar-free mints when you feel like munching between meals. You can also keep your hands and mouth busy with a toothpick or a straw.";
"quit_symptom_appetite_moving_heading" = "Get moving";
"quit_symptom_appetite_moving_body" = "You don't need to hit the gym, even a simple walk is great for your health.";
"quit_symptom_appetite_focus_heading" = "Focus on eating";
"quit_symptom_appetite_focus_body" = "When you eat, try to pay attention to what you're eating. Turn off the TV or put away your phone. Eating a bit slower and really savoring your food can help you notice when you're full.";
"quit_symptom_sleep_intro" = "After you quit smoking, you might find it a bit tricky to get a good night's sleep at first. But don't worry, it gets better! If it's really bothering you, talk to your healthcare provider—they can help. Because if you're super tired from not sleeping well, it can make it tougher to stay smoke-free.\n\nHere's how you can manage it:";
"quit_symptom_sleep_caffeine_heading" = "Watch your caffeine";
"quit_symptom_sleep_caffeine_body" = "If you're into coffee, tea, or other caffeinated drinks, avoid them in the late afternoon or evening. After quitting smoking, caffeine sticks around longer in your body.";
"quit_symptom_sleep_patch_heading" = "Nicotine patch tip";
"quit_symptom_sleep_patch_body" = "If you're using the nicotine patch, try taking it off an hour before bedtime. Sometimes, nicotine from the patch can mess with your sleep.";
"quit_symptom_sleep_space_heading" = "Create a sleep-friendly space";
"quit_symptom_sleep_space_body" = "No TV, phones, or gadgets in bed. Make your bedroom quiet, dark, relaxing, and comfy.\nSkip heavy meals or alcohol right before bedtime.";
"quit_symptom_sleep_active_heading" = "Stay active";
"quit_symptom_sleep_active_body" = "Do some physical activity during the day (just not right before bed).";
"quit_symptom_sleep_routine_heading" = "Sleep routine";
"quit_symptom_sleep_routine_body" = "Try going to bed and waking up around the same time every day, even on weekends.";
"quit_symptom_depression_intro" = "People who smoke are more likely to have anxiety or depression than people who don't smoke. Some people feel mood changes for a short time after they quit smoking. For some people, smoking may seem like it helps with anxiety or depression, but don't be tricked. Smoking might make you feel better in the short-term, but that's because the nicotine in cigarettes stops the discomfort of withdrawal, and not because it is helping with anxiety or depression. There are much better ways to deal with withdrawal symptoms and mood changes than returning to smoking! The good news is that once people have been smoke-free for a few months, their anxiety and depression levels are often lower than when they were smoking.\n\nHere's how you can manage it:";
"quit_symptom_depression_moving_heading" = "Get moving";
"quit_symptom_depression_moving_body" = "Exercise can really boost your mood. Start with something small and work your way up. It might be tough when you're feeling low, but it's worth it!";
"quit_symptom_depression_busy_heading" = "Stay busy";
"quit_symptom_depression_busy_body" = "Keep yourself occupied and have a routine. Even getting out of the house for a bit can make a difference.";
"quit_symptom_depression_connect_heading" = "Connect with others";
"quit_symptom_depression_connect_body" = "Talk to people who support you in quitting smoking. Having someone to chat with every day can lift your spirits.";
"quit_symptom_depression_treat_heading" = "Treat yourself";
"quit_symptom_depression_treat_body" = "Do things you enjoy, even if they're small. Those little things can make you feel better.";
"quit_symptom_depression_talk_heading" = "Talk to someone";
"quit_symptom_depression_talk_body" = "If you're still not feeling great after a couple of weeks or if it's just too much, reach out to a healthcare provider.\nYou call 1-800-QUIT-NOW, you can speak confidentially with a highly trained quit coach.";
"quit_symptom_depression_stress_heading" = "Explore ways to handle stress and anxiety";
"quit_symptom_depression_discovery_body" = "Have you checked out \"discovery\"? There are lots of useful courses that can help you manage stress and anxiety!";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_title" = "Feeling Jumpy or Restless";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_intro" = "When you first quit smoking, it's totally normal to feel a bit jumpy or restless. It's like your body is adjusting without nicotine, and it can affect not just your mind but the rest of you too.\n\nHere's how you can handle it:";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_move_heading" = "Move around:";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_move_body" = "Doing some physical activity can really help with that jumpy feeling. Take a short walk or just get up and stretch if you're feeling restless.";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_caffeine_heading" = "Watch your caffeine";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_caffeine_body" = "If you're into coffee, tea, or other caffeinated drinks, try cutting back a bit. After quitting smoking, caffeine hangs around in your body longer.";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_success_heading" = "Increase your chance to success";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_timeline_schedule" = "Schedule the date when you will quit. Make it soon, preferably within a month, so you will still be excited to become tobacco-free. Cut back on your tobacco use by one cigarette every two days before you completely give up tobacco on your quit date. Aim to start quitting completely when you are down to at least 10 cigarettes per day.";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_timeline_quit_day" = "When your quit day arrives, get rid of all your cigarettes and/or chewing tobacco.";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_timeline_clean" = "Clean your house to remove the odour of tobacco from your clothes, furnitures and car.";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_timeline_ashtrays" = "Get rid of ashtrays, lighters and matches.";
"quit_symptom_jumpy_timeline_medication" = "Consider using a nicotine replacement patch and/or tobacco-cessation medication.";
